<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khanty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ostyaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khanty, formerly called Ostyaks, are a Finno-Ugric people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, living mainly along the Ob and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> and their tributaries in the Khanty-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a> Autonomous Okrug. Traditionally hunters, fishers and reindeer herders, they paid fur tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-sibir">Khanate of Sibir</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Their language is close to Mansi and, more distantly, related to Hungarian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khanty.jpg" alt="Khanty" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dmitry Afonin · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostyak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the second millennium BCE the lands between the Kama and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> were home to a Proto-Uralic-speaking population that had contacts with Proto-Indo-European speakers to the south. The woodland people of this region are held to be ancestors of the Ugrian inhabitants of Trans-Uralia, though other researchers place the Khanty origin in the southern Ural steppe, from which they moved north about 500 CE. The Khanty probably appear in Russian records as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugra">Yugra</a> around the 11th century, when they dealt with hunters and merchants from Novgorod. The name Ostyak comes from the Khanty words for a person from the Ob.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugra">Yugra</a> was a name for numerous tribes, each with its own centre and chief, and each divided into two exogamic phratries whose members counted as blood relatives. Clans later replaced this structure, and each clan leader, called a knyazets, negotiated with the Russian realm. Khanty princes joined Russian campaigns and received the right to collect yasaq, or tribute, from two Khanty districts, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> withdrew these privileges when they were no longer needed. Some Khanty princedoms were partly included in the Siberia Khanate from the 1440s to the 1570s.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-conquest-of-siberia">Russian conquest of Siberia</a>, missionaries and officials ordered idols destroyed, held mass baptisms, punished those who disobeyed the church and took Khanty children as hostages to be converted. Conversions were largely superficial and driven by economic incentives, so Native practices continued within Khanty belief. In the Soviet period the Khanty were among the few Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to receive an autonomous okrug. In the 1930s the state moved to collectivise them, executing tribal chiefs as kulaks and then shamans. Children were taken to Russian-speaking boarding schools, which provoked the Kazym rebellion of 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Khanty traditionally lived by fishing, taiga hunting and reindeer herding, and as trappers they relied heavily on gathering. In winter they lived in stationary huts of earth and branches in permanent villages. In spring they moved to hunting and fishing grounds and built temporary shelters of birch bark and poles. Most Khanty today are Orthodox Christians who combine this with traditional beliefs. The Bear Celebration is held after a successful bear hunt and lasts five or six days, with over 300 songs and performances. Khanty literature began in the first half of the 20th century, and their language belongs to the Ugric branch of the Uralic family, closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a> and Hungarian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khanty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sibir ruled Siberian Tatars and tribute-paying forest peoples such as the <b>Khanty</b> and Mansi, whose fur tribute was its main wealth.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-sibir">Khanate of Sibir</a></li></ul></section>
